<BlogPostLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  category="career-options"
  publishedAt={publishedDateISO}
  publishedLabel={publishedDateLabel}
  authorName={authorName}
  authorUrl={authorUrl}
  authorRole={authorRole}
  jsonLdExtras={[faqJsonLd]}
>
  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>PCM career options with salary do not have one answer. A government survey puts the median engineering fresher in formal work at Rs 25,000 a month, while the best-paid tracks in the same field pay several times that.</strong>
    What separates the two is rarely the degree name. It is the route, the employer, and a high-income skill portfolio built on top, which is how the right early decisions unlock higher income and earlier financial freedom.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Formal engineering pay in India rises from a median of Rs 25,000 a month at entry to Rs 42,000 at three or more years (PLFS 2025). Only 11.2% earn Rs 50,000 a month or more at entry, and 42.9% do by year three.</li>
      <li>The spread is bigger than the growth. Software base pay runs from Rs 3.5 lakh to Rs 20 lakh between the 10th and 90th percentile, so route, employer and proof matter more than waiting.</li>
      <li><strong>Lead with routes you can scale: software, data, and architecture or core engineering with a business layer. Treat PSU, lab and research posts as capped ladders, and keep a skill running beside them.</strong></li>
      <li>Before you trust any number, ask four things: who counted, out of how many, which employer type, and which year. We call this The 4-Question Pay Check.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p>
    This guide looks only at pay. For the work-style map of every PCM route, read
    <a href="/blog/career-options/pcm-career-options/">PCM career options</a>. For the full library, see
    <a href="/blog/career-options/">career options</a>. If you want help turning one of these routes into a plan that fits your money and strengths, see
    <a href="/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/career-counselling-for-pcm-students/">career counselling for PCM students</a>.
  </p>

  <nav class="jump-nav" aria-label="Article sections">
    <p class="jump-nav__label">Jump to section</p>
    <ul class="jump-nav__list">
      {jumpLinks.map((item) => (
        <li><a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.label}</a></li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="claims">How to read a PCM salary claim: The 4-Question Pay Check</h2>

  <p>
    Most PCM salary pages print a range and move on. The range hides who was counted and when.
  </p>

  <p>
    Run every number through The 4-Question Pay Check before you plan around it.
  </p>

  <div class="protocol-stack">
    {questionItems.map((item) => (
      <div class="checkpoint">
        <div class="checkpoint__num">{item.num}</div>
        <div class="checkpoint__body">
          <div class="checkpoint__title">{item.title}</div>
          <p>{item.body}</p>
          <div class="checkpoint__note">{item.note}</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2 id="table">PCM career options with salary, route by route, ordered by growth headroom</h2>

  <p>
    The order below is not prestige order or exam-difficulty order. It leads with the routes where pay can grow beyond a salary scale, then moves to routes where the ladder is fixed. Every route carries one of three labels: Scalable, Scalable with a business layer, or Not so scalable.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table salary-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Route and label</th>
          <th>Fresher</th>
          <th>Around year five</th>
          <th>Top band</th>
          <th>How far to trust it</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {routeRows.map((r) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Route and label">
              <strong>{r.route}</strong>
