            <td data-label="First skill worth learning">{r.first}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Start with people you already know. Referrals and specific, warm messages beat mass cold outreach. Give something useful first, such as a short fix or a clear question about their work, before you ask for anything.
  </p>

  <ol class="rich-list">
    {priceSteps.map((s) => (
      <li><strong>{s.title}</strong> {s.body}</li>
    ))}
  </ol>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      <strong>The most scalable ownership path for a PCM student is selling a software, data or design skill directly.</strong> It scales because you set the price, can package repeat work into a fixed offer, reach clients in any city, and can later hire help.
    </p>
    <p>
      A registered architecture practice and a small engineering service are slower versions of the same idea. None of this is guaranteed. It needs proof, follow-up and patience, and you can start while you hold a job. Do not rely on one marketplace or one client: keep a client list you own. Once you invoice, check current GST and Udyam rules on the official portals.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="ceiling">Real ceiling or capped salary ladder</h2>

  <p>
    This is the question a pay number cannot answer. A route has a real ceiling when you can raise your own price, hire help or sell something repeatedly. It has a capped ladder when a scale or an exam count sets your pay.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    {ceilingCards.map((card) => (
      <div class={`compare-card compare-card--${card.tone}`}>
