          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    If you ask whether a better adjacent route exists, one does for many students. A PCM student who likes the work of software but fears the crowded entry can aim for data or product skills in a domain they know. Domain depth plus tool skill is harder to copy than either one alone.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cost">College cost, debt and the pay math</h2>

  <p>
    A pay number only means something next to what you paid to reach it. Use a simple test: divide the four-year fee by the monthly entry pay.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Illustrative four-year fee</th>
          <th>Months of Rs 25,000 entry pay</th>
          <th>Whole budget at the 10% line</th>
          <th>Whole budget at the 20% caution line</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {feeRows.map((r) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Illustrative four-year fee"><strong>{r.fee}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Months of Rs 25,000 entry pay">{r.months}</td>
            <td data-label="Whole budget at the 10% line">{r.ten}</td>
            <td data-label="Whole budget at the 20% caution line">{r.twenty}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    The fees are examples for the working, not quotes. Months use gross pay, so real take-home time is longer. The 10% and 20% lines are strict planning rules, not laws.
  </p>

  <p>
    College is a small part of career success. Keep about 10% of your family's total education budget for college, and ask for course-specific evidence above about 20%. The other 90% pays for living costs, a laptop, current learning, projects, exams and the upskilling that raises pay after the degree. Avoid education debt unless a credential is legally required. An engineering or architecture degree is needed for those licences and most jobs, but it is not a full skill system.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">If an MS abroad is in the plan</p>
    <p>
      Treat a large study-abroad loan as high risk, because repayment can depend on a visa, a foreign job and the exchange rate. Consider it only when the programme is among the strongest in the world for your subject and the advantage is verified, and use "top five" as a strict screen, not a promise.
    </p>
    <p>
      Compare it with a debt-light route at home. Do not count a hoped-for scholarship, and check its renewal terms. When you compare salaries across countries, subtract tax, rent and loan payments first.
    </p>
  </div>

